 DR, playback, body-wrench and fixed-variant capability sources remain their existing instance APIs. The static inventory intentionally leaves those dependent entries unknown; `backend.get_capabilities()` aggregates authoritative instance declarations. Multiple logical entity partitions do not imply arbitrary multi-articulation composition. URDF investigations and unmerged branches are not current support. IsaacSim's legacy reserved zero contact buffer never means a valid contact query or absence of physical contact; mapped scenes serve declared geom-pair `contact data="force" reduce="netforce"` sensors through the dedicated PhysX pair-force slot and wildcard body-net force (`geom2` omitted) plus body-net `data="found"` flags through one batched per-entity PhysX contact view, while legacy model-file scenes fail all contact declarations closed. Isaac worker sensors support gyro reconstruction but reject accelerometers.
 
 The [capability design decision](adr-capabilities.md) defines evidence matching and snapshot lifetime. `available` in the installation table above is never a task compatibility decision.
+
+### TGS external force application (DexLab adapter extension)
+
+`isaacsim_external_forces_every_iteration=True` applies external forces at each native TGS position iteration via `PhysxSceneAPI`. The default `None` preserves SDK behavior; explicit `False` disables the option. The host and worker require an actual boolean and verify the USD readback. This changes force application timing, not the integration timestep or engine source. Validate affected dynamics before adoption; existing saved experiments retain their original configuration.
+
+### Mesh-backed SDF transport (DexLab adapter extension)
+
+The mapped IsaacSim path preserves MJCF `type="sdf"` intent separately from importer transport. A temporary sibling MJCF spells only mesh-backed SDF geoms as `mesh`; the native importer requires adjacent relative mesh resources and a USD-compatible filename. Role authoring then applies `PhysxSDFMeshCollisionAPI` with resolution 256 and sparse subgrid resolution 6. Native USD readback rejects missing SDF APIs or changed approximation/resolution. Both artifact identities include the transport version; role identities also include per-geom SDF intent. Optional absent geom slots remain collision-disabled placeholders. Plugin SDFs without a mesh are rejected. Ordinary mesh collision semantics are unchanged.
+
+This is a new SDF sampled from the source triangle surface, not a transfer of MuJoCo octree samples or equivalent contact mechanics. USD readback alone is not contact qualification: the DexLab torus/sphere protocol tests an open hole, support on the torus surface and an explicit convex-hull negative control. Official PhysX and IsaacLab sources are unchanged.
+
+### Native contact diagnostics (DexLab adapter extension)
+
+For one mapped environment, `backend.enable_contact_details("object", capacity=8192)` enables diagnostics for a declared single-body rigid entity already covered by a contact sensor. Enable it before recorded stepping. `backend.get_contact_details()` returns the last physics substep's normal contacts and separate friction anchors, with public target body IDs, world-frame positions, forces in N and separation in m. They are not one-to-one records. Both channels must be summed for complete contact force; ordinary pair sensors retain their existing normal-only semantics.
+
+The native buffers are polled on every substep, even when a host call advances several steps; only the last snapshot is returned. Record with one step per call for complete time coverage. A buffer reaching capacity, invalid slices, overlapping ranges or nonfinite active rows fails the operation. This diagnostic does not score success, change contact parameters or alter engine source. Native metadata additionally reads the composed USD `physics:filteredPairs` relationships; callers must compare them with their intended collision model, including any implicit source-engine rules.
+
+### Explicit torsional contact radius
+
+`create_backend("isaacsim", ..., isaacsim_min_torsional_patch_radius=radius)` optionally sets the official PhysX minimum torsional patch radius, in meters, on every composed collision shape before initialization. `None` preserves defaults; zero explicitly disables this minimum-radius contribution. Values must be finite and nonnegative, and host/worker validate the request and uniform native readback. Cached role files remain unchanged.
+
+This is an explicit contact-model assumption, not an automatic conversion of MuJoCo torsional friction coefficients or a material calibration. Native normal/friction point records do not expose a separate torsional impulse; linear force closure is not a full angular-momentum check. PhysX SDK source and binaries are unchanged.
+
+### DexLab local compliant contact
+
+Explicit `isaacsim_compliant_contact_stiffness` and `isaacsim_compliant_contact_damping` must be provided together and be finite/nonnegative. Zero stiffness disables compliance and requires zero damping. Positive stiffness selects native force-based implicit spring contact (N/m and N s/m per native contact constraint); the adapter fixes stiffness/damping combination to average and disables acceleration-spring semantics. All enabled collision materials must be bound and consistent. Values are authored after cached roles are composed and before reset, then read from composed USD; this is not a direct native material-tensor readback or a measured macroscopic modulus. Defaults remain unchanged. This is a local DexLab adapter extension, not an upstream release.
+
+### DexLab local inertial transport precision
+
+Worker source export writes compiled body mass, COM, diagonal inertia and inertia-frame quaternion with 17 significant digits after `MjSpec.to_xml()`. This avoids its six-digit rounding, including for inferred and full-tensor source inertia. The native engine can still round to its own dtype; no solver or SDK is modified. Geometry, joint and other serialized fields are outside this fix. Native readback remains required.
